
  [1mThe fork:[0m where the identity comes from determines what it [3mis[0m, and that's the hardest thing to change later — it lands
  in storage, in the function signature, and in every future form that reads it.

  - [1mServer-issued[0m — the backend returns an id on successful login. It's authoritative and stable across devices, and
    it's the only option that genuinely identifies a [3mperson[0m. Costs: nothing exists to return it ([38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m at
    [38;5;153mapp.js:2[39m is a stub returning a hardcoded object), so this depends on backend work outside this repo. Also means
    there's no id at all until after a successful login.
  - [1mClient-generated[0m — the app mints an id (e.g. a UUID) on first visit and reuses it. Works today with no backend, and
    exists before login, so pre-auth forms can use it. Costs: it identifies a [3mbrowser[0m, not a person. Clearing storage or
    switching devices makes a new one. If you later add server identity, you have two id spaces to reconcile.
  - [1mDerived from the username[0m — no new concept, no storage question. Cheapest by far, but it isn't really an id: it
    changes if a username changes, it's not opaque, and putting it in persistent storage and logs means writing
    user-entered strings into both.

  [1mMy recommendation: client-generated,[0m given that the backend here is a stub. It's the only one that works end-to-end
  today and is available before login, which matters if other forms need it. The reconciliation cost against future
  server identity is real but containable if we keep it in one module with a narrow interface.
